---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';

const TITLE = 'Kinetic — Remember the email, not the exact words';
const DESCRIPTION =
  'A local-first Gmail workspace for semantic search, sender intelligence, and filter recipes. Your emails and embeddings stay in your browser.';
---

<Layout title={TITLE} description={DESCRIPTION}>
  <main>
    <nav class="nav shell" aria-label="Primary navigation">
      <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Kinetic home">
        <span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">K</span>
        <span>Kinetic</span>
      </a>
      <div class="nav-links">
        <a href="#inside">Product</a>
        <a href="#privacy">Privacy</a>
        <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>
        <a class="button button-small" href="/app">Open app</a>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <section class="hero">
      <div class="shell hero-grid">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="kicker">Local-first inbox intelligence</p>
          <h1>Remember the email.<br /><em>Not the exact words.</em></h1>
          <p class="hero-lede">
            Search Gmail by meaning, see which senders fill your week, and turn recurring noise into durable filters. The model, messages, and search index stay in your browser.
          </p>
          <div class="hero-actions">
            <a class="button" href="/app">Connect Gmail and search</a>
            <a class="text-link" href="#inside">See the product <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
          </div>
          <p class="hero-meta">Read-only Gmail · No server-side mailbox copy · In-browser ONNX</p>
        </div>

        <div class="search-stage" aria-label="Kinetic semantic search product preview">
          <div class="browser-chrome">
            <span></span><span></span><span></span>
            <p>kinetic / semantic search</p>
          </div>
          <div class="search-workspace">
            <div class="workspace-rail" aria-hidden="true">
              <span class="rail-brand">K</span>
              <span class="rail-item active"></span>
              <span class="rail-item"></span>
              <span class="rail-item"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="workspace-main">
              <p class="workspace-label">Search by meaning</p>
              <div class="query-box">
                <span aria-hidden="true">⌕</span>
                <strong>flight confirmation from last summer</strong>
                <kbd>↵</kbd>
              </div>
              <div class="result featured">
                <span class="score">91%</span>
                <div><strong>United Airlines</strong><p>Your trip to SFO — boarding pass for Jul 14</p></div>
              </div>
              <div class="result">
                <span class="score">87%</span>
                <div><strong>Airbnb</strong><p>Reservation confirmed: Mission, San Francisco</p></div>
              </div>
              <div class="result">
                <span class="score">82%</span>
                <div><strong>Google Calendar</strong><p>Flight UA 215 — Wed, Jul 14, 6:40 AM</p></div>
              </div>
              <p class="local-note"><span aria-hidden="true">●</span> Ranked locally. Nothing uploaded.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="inside" class="proof-section">
      <div class="shell section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Look inside</p>
          <h2>A working inbox cockpit.<br />Not a privacy promise pasted on later.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Kinetic pairs three jobs that normally live in separate tools: recall an email, understand the senders behind the volume, and make Gmail quieter.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="shell proof-grid">
        <article class="proof-card proof-search">
          <p class="chapter-number">01 / Recall</p>
          <h3>Describe what you remember.</h3>
          <p>“The contractor quote before Diwali” works even when the subject line does not. Embeddings are generated and compared on-device.</p>
          <div class="mini-search"><span>⌕</span><strong>the lease renewal from last spring</strong></div>
        </article>
        <article class="proof-card proof-volume">
          <p class="chapter-number">02 / Patterns</p>
          <h3>See who owns your inbox.</h3>
          <p>Sender analytics turns an abstract feeling of overload into a ranked, inspectable list.</p>
          <div class="volume-list" aria-label="Example sender volume">
            <span><strong>Product updates</strong><i style="--w: 88%"></i><b>42</b></span>
            <span><strong>Shopping</strong><i style="--w: 64%"></i><b>31</b></span>
            <span><strong>Travel</strong><i style="--w: 36%"></i><b>17</b></span>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="proof-card proof-rules">
          <p class="chapter-number">03 / Rules</p>
          <h3>Turn recognition into relief.</h3>
          <p>Review suggested Gmail filters, export them, and let the rules work when Kinetic is closed.</p>
          <div class="rule-chip"><span>from:(updates@example.com)</span><strong>Skip inbox</strong></div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="tension shell">
      <p class="statement">Your inbox remembers everything. Ordinary search asks you to remember the wording.</p>
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">The mismatch</p>
        <h2>Human memory is contextual.</h2>
        <p>We remember the trip, the person, or the season. Kinetic searches that way—without first building a second copy of your mailbox on somebody else’s server.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="fit-section">
      <div class="shell fit-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Know before you connect</p>
          <h2>A sharp fit,<br />not a universal inbox.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="fit-copy">
          <p><strong>A fit</strong>You use Gmail, want better recall and sender visibility, and prefer intelligence that runs locally.</p>
          <p><strong>Not a fit</strong>You need a shared team mailbox, server-side archival, automated replies, or actions beyond a read-only connection.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy" class="privacy-section">
      <div class="shell privacy-grid">
        <div class="zero-mark" aria-hidden="true"><span>0</span><small>mailbox bytes<br />stored on server</small></div>
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Privacy is architecture</p>
          <h2>Your inbox stays yours.</h2>
          <p>Messages, cached bodies, embeddings, and the semantic index live in IndexedDB in your browser. Kinetic’s server keeps only the authentication records needed for the read-only Gmail connection and proxies live Gmail calls.</p>
          <a class="text-link light-link" href="/privacy">Read the plain-language privacy policy <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq shell" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Honest answers</p>
        <h2 id="faq-title">Before you hand any app your inbox.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details open>
          <summary>Can Kinetic send, archive, or delete email?</summary>
          <p>No. The Gmail connection is read-only. Kinetic cannot compose, send, archive, or delete messages.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Where does semantic search run?</summary>
          <p>Inside your browser with HuggingFace Transformers and ONNX. The resulting embeddings stay in IndexedDB on that device.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What happens when I clear browser storage?</summary>
          <p>Your local index and cached mailbox data are removed. Kinetic must rebuild them from Gmail after you reconnect.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does Kinetic replace Gmail?</summary>
          <p>No. It is a focused workspace for semantic recall, sender analysis, unsubscribe help, and filter recipes on top of Gmail.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="principle-section">
      <div class="shell principle-grid">
        <blockquote>“Useful enough to understand your inbox. Constrained enough not to take it over.”</blockquote>
        <div><p>Kinetic’s read-only boundary is part of the product, not a beta limitation. Destructive mailbox actions do not belong here.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closing-section">
      <div class="shell">
        <p class="kicker">Try your own memory</p>
        <h2>Find the email<br />you can almost describe.</h2>
        <a class="button" href="/app">Connect Gmail and search</a>
        <p class="closing-note">Read-only access. Local index. Disconnect whenever you want.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="site-footer shell">
      <div>
        <a class="brand" href="/"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true">K</span><span>Kinetic</span></a>
        <p>Semantic Gmail search and sender intelligence that runs in your browser.</p>
      </div>
      <nav aria-label="Footer navigation">
        <a href="/about">About</a><a href="/privacy">Privacy</a><a href="/terms">Terms</a><a href="/faq">FAQ</a><a href="/changelog">Changelog</a><a href="/app">Open app</a>
      </nav>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>
